# THE LONG RUN — 20M transitions, the first time this project has ever trained past ~4.4M.
#   supervisor.py --queue scripts/tdmpc/long_queue.jsonl --run_tag long --grade_plant baseline
#     --grade_horizon 6 --grade_warm_start --no_early_stop --max_env_steps 20000000
#
# WHY NOW. The H=6 fleet (2026-08-15) ended with NOTHING plateaued. Speed slope over 2-3M was
# +0.030..+0.069 m/s per 1M in all four arms, and the new in-training fall telemetry fell
# monotonically to the budget wall without flattening:
#     falls/min   0.0M  0.5M  1.0M  1.5M  2.0M  2.5M   slope 2-3M
#     h6-flat     2.51  2.31  2.15  2.03  1.89  1.55     -0.612   <- still ACCELERATING downward
#     h6-base-rep 2.34  2.06  2.02  1.90  1.79  1.71     -0.172
#     h6-utd1     2.40  2.25  2.17  1.89  1.77  1.70     -0.227
# Falling is still being trained out at the moment every run so far has been stopped. Separately:
# every 10M/20M-budget run in this project's history was killed at ~2.1M by plateau rules keyed on
# signals the fall audit showed are blind, so "TD-MPC2 plateaus here" has never actually been tested.
#
# CONFIG = the fleet's measured winner (h6-flat, fitness 0.0936, falls 12.5%, tilt 6.5 deg -- better
# posture than the PPO reference's 9.7) with ONE addition, plus every choice the fleet settled:
#   horizon 6            H=6-trained arms cut falls 18.8% -> 12.5-17.2% vs the H=3-trained lean-C.
#   flat_orientation -0.5  THE win. vs its matched seed (h6-base-rep): falls 14.8->12.5, tilt
#                        9.5->6.5. Kept at -0.5, the value actually measured, not raised to PPO's
#                        -2.183 -- one long run is no place to extrapolate an untested weight.
#   soft gate 0.95/0.15  lean-C's, carried by every arm.
#   updates_per_step 16  UTD 1.0 was tested (h6-utd1) and was the WORST arm, 0.0523. Staying at
#                        UTD 0.5 saves ~40% of the wall clock at no measured cost.
#   NO track_lin_vel_xy  Take 1 tried this at 0.5 to price the overshoot and it had to be killed
#                        at 1.54M. track_lin_vel_xy_yaw_frame_exp is exp(-err^2/std^2) with NO
#                        uprightness or height factor, so a COLLAPSED robot sliding at the command
#                        earns the full weight. That inverts the env's design rule (gated core is
#                        the main positive; additive positives must stay small): a collapsed robot
#                        went from -0.35/step to +0.15/step. Measured: ep_len 500 -> 444 and
#                        FALLING (every prior run held exactly 500) while return rose 14.4 -> 17.4.
#                        The overshoot is real but must be priced with an additive-NEGATIVE term
#                        (penalise speed ABOVE command) or a GATED tracking term -- a separate
#                        experiment, not a passenger on a 46 h run.
#   buffer_size 10M      3.3 GB VRAM (of 16, with ~4.9 used by Isaac+model+MPPI). Holds the 400k
#                        demo resident for the first ~10M steps. A no-eviction buffer for 20M would
#                        need 20.4M rows = 6.75 GB and risks OOM; upstream evicts constantly at 1M.
#   --no_early_stop      only DIVERGENCE (pi_loss>2.5) may cut this. See above.
#
# COST ~46 h at the fleet's measured 121 steps/s. Checkpoints every 50k steps, so stopping early
# from TensorBoard costs nothing -- grade the latest model_<N>.pt.
# WATCH, in priority order:
#   collect/mean_episode_len  MUST stay pinned at 500. Anything below means hard_collapse is firing,
#                             which no healthy run in this project has ever done. This is the canary
#                             that caught take 1 at 1.5M -- check it first, every time.
#   collect/falls_per_min     the reason for the run; should keep declining past 3M.
#   collect/ground_speed_mps  vs the 0.300 command. Overshoot is expected (h6-flat ended at 1.23x)
#                             and is NOT priced in this config -- that is a deliberate, known gap.
#   collect/torso_lean_deg    should sit within ~10 deg of 0.
#   loss/pi_loss              >1.5 sustained = value overoptimism (the one stopper still armed).
# RETURN IS NOT A HEALTH SIGNAL. Take 1's return was the HIGHEST ever recorded while it was
# collapsing in ~22% of episodes. Read ep_len and falls_per_min, never return alone.
{"name": "long-20M-v2", "entry": "bootstrap.py", "warm_start": "scratch", "variant": "walk-biped-tdmpc", "max_env_steps": 20000000, "seed": 21, "flags": ["--plant", "baseline", "--cmd_vx", "0.3", "--seed_transitions", "400000", "--pretrain_updates", "20000", "--bc_plan_std", "0.15", "--buffer_size", "10000000", "--horizon", "6", "--mppi_warm_start"], "overrides": {"rewards.stand_walk.params.upright_min": 0.95, "rewards.stand_walk.params.upright_margin": 0.15, "rewards.flat_orientation_l2.weight": -0.5}, "notes": "The full-length run, take 2. h6-flat's measured config EXACTLY -- take 1 added track_lin_vel_xy 0.5 and had to be killed at 1.54M: that term is UNGATED and POSITIVE, so a collapsed robot sliding at the commanded speed earned the full 0.5/step. ep_len fell 500 -> 444 and declining while return ROSE 14.4 -> 17.4. Nothing untested goes into this run. Question: does the walk settle if we finally let it run? Best so far is 12.5% of episodes containing a fall against the PPO reference's 6.2%."}
